  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 205 AM EST Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Much colder air persists this weekend into \nearly next week. Near-freezing temperatures will be possible \neach night through Monday morning. Below normal temperatures \ncontinue through Tuesday, with warmer readings expected for mid\nto late next week.\n\nLatest analysis reveals a broad upper low centered over New\nEngland into the coastal Northeast, with deepening low pressure\nSSE of Cape Cod. To the west, 1028+mb sfc high pressure\ncontinues to slide east from the Northern Plains toward the\nupper midwest. \n\nEarly morning WV/IR composite imagery shows a weak shortwave \nrounding the base of the upper trough as it crosses the \nCarolinas. Weak overrunning moisture associated with this \nfeature has produced a narrow band of light rain just south of \nthe local area early this morning, with our area once again\nremaining mainly dry. Lingering mid-level clouds are slowly \nclearing across our southern tier of counties into eastern NC \ndue to subsidence behind the departing shortwave. Meanwhile, a \nlingering stratus/stratocumulus deck over the Lower Eastern \nShore is holding temperatures in the upper 30s to lower 40s. \nFarther inland, between these two features, partial clearing \nhas allowed temperatures to fall into the lower to mid 30s as of\nthis writing over much of central and south- central VA. The \nFreeze Warning remains in effect through 8 AM over the I-95 \ncorridor, mainly along and south of the RIC metro and extending\nto the inland portion of the VA Northern Neck south to\nNorthampton and Hertford counties in NC. A Frost Advisory \nremains in place over the coastal Northern Neck along the \neastern flank of the Freeze Warning. It remains to be seen how \ntemperatures fare over the next few hours and how much of the \narea will have its growing season ended by a light freeze. Will\nallow the day crew to evaluate morning observations before \naddressing frost/freeze headlines for tonight and Sunday night. \nTonight will bring another opportunity for a similar light \nfreeze over this area, followed by a more classically favorable \nradiational cooling setup Sunday night into early Monday with \nclear skies and decoupling winds as chilly high pressure slides \nover the region.\n\nOtherwise, minimal sensible weather concerns are expected over\nthe next several days. Cooler, drier conditions persist well \ninto next week. Temperatures will average 8-10 degrees below \nnormal, with highs in the 50s to low 60s and lows in the upper \n20s to low 30s inland (mid 30s to low 40s along the coast).",
